<section id="start" class="chapter"><div class="chapter-title"><span class="index">01</span><div><h2>Your first working loop</h2><p class="deck">Build a small thing. Keep the feedback close.</p></div></div>
<p>Start with the included <strong>Signal Notebook</strong>: a command-line program that reads integer scores, validates them, and reports their mean. It has no third-party dependencies. You can follow every value from file input through a typed domain model to output.</p>
<Code label="shell" code={"cd starter    # inside the rust-studio repository\n# If this shell cannot find Cargo:\nsource \"$HOME/.cargo/env\"\ncargo run -- scores.txt\ncargo test\ncargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo fmt --check"} />
<p>Expected program output, as recorded by the last verified build: <code>{verified.starter.stdout.trim()}</code>. The full source is on the <a href="/starter/">starter page</a>. Open the <code>starter</code> folder in Zed. Rust support is built in; this project asks rust-analyzer to run Clippy for saved-file diagnostics. The terminal workflow works independently of editor setup.</p>
<div class="split"><div><h3>Know the four names</h3><dl><dt>rustup</dt><dd>Selects and installs toolchains and targets.</dd><dt>rustc</dt><dd>Compiles Rust source.</dd><dt>Cargo</dt><dd>Builds packages, resolves dependencies, runs tests and generates documentation.</dd><dt>rust-analyzer</dt><dd>Provides completion, navigation and editor diagnostics.</dd></dl></div><aside><h3>Three useful distinctions</h3><p>A <strong>package</strong> is described by Cargo.toml. A <strong>crate</strong> is a compilation unit, such as a library or binary. A <strong>module</strong> organizes names inside a crate.</p><p><code>cargo check</code> gives fast compiler feedback; <code>cargo build</code> also produces build artifacts. Use <code>--release</code> for performance measurements.</p></aside></div>
<p class="cite">Read the source: <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/">The Rust Programming Language</a> · <a href="https://zed.dev/docs/languages/rust">Zed · Rust</a></p></section>
